[{:seon.task/name "tests-fork-from-the-branchs-warm-agent"
  :seon.task/plan "DECIDED 2026-11-17 (owner and root; decisions.md#2026-11-17-rulings; docs/plan.md#where-things-stand, the test model): a branch's tests fork from its clean base, never from its agent. The clean base is a fork of the fork server (which only ever load-diff!s main's commits and runs no user's or agent's eval) brought by load-diff! to the exact commit the merge would make (the branch with main merged); it is kept while the branch head holds and dropped when it moves, found by key (see seon.db/agents-environments-runtimes-and-builds-are-four-entities). Nothing else runs in it; investigating the runtime happens in a fork of it. A test needs an environment and a runtime, never an agent (docs/data-modeling.md#agents-environments-runtimes-and-builds, the four kinds of test): the harness takes the environment under test, forks its clean base, binds that environment as the branch context the agent API reads, and runs the test; a test needing a stored branch gets a child environment, and only a test whose subject is an agent spawns one. The design below is kept where it agrees. PROCESS TESTS MEASURED (tests2, 2026-11-16, result/ce824519, by the process var each reaches): 160 of ~2,225. 103 through seon.agent-test/in-agent (they boot a shard's root: the candidate users, each takes the booted candidate as an argument for F2); 40 through seon.lib/fork! alone (forks of the test process; several end their process group or parent, so they stay process tests: run at an agent's REPL they took the standing system down, 2026-11-16); 17 through seon.test-store/binary-sh (run the built binary: the boot, resume and hand-over tests of seon.system-test, seon.lib-test's three fork cases, six seon.agent-test runs, one each in repl, server and test-store; each greeting-only one is deleted, the rest run on the candidate's binary). SPEC FOR FLOW (root and the owner, 2026-11-16: tests run in a flow; the candidate is the prove proc's state, each batch a message it handles; flow builds it after its audit of our Flow code; no global var, no per-run value in config). INPUTS: the judge's plan of a change (seon.agent/test-plan: :run, :process, :digests) reaching process tests; the candidate commit C (the branch with main merged). STATE of the prove proc: at most one booted candidate per C, {:commit C :port :branch :binary :digest}, made by one call of seon.test-store/candidate (one build of C when a build input moved, else the standing binary, booted once on a clone of the process store, in root's process: never in an agent or a test child); replaced when C moves; stopped when no batch of C is left. MESSAGE: a batch {:items {test digest} :candidate {:port :branch}} granted to the agent holding C's branch; the in-process and process items of one judge run go out together, never a process item to a checkout. RAIL (root, 2026-11-16, after a fork! test run at an agent took 7888 down): each process-test child leads its own process group (setsid at its fork), so a test that ends its group or its parent ends only itself, never the system it was forked from; proof on the candidate, never 7888: seon.lib-test/a-process-that-leads-its-group-ends-the-group run as an item leaves the candidate and its siblings running. THE AGENT'S PART: a granted process item runs in its test child against the batch's candidate, never refused as not-run. HOW THE CHILD HOLDS IT (root, 2026-11-16): the candidate is a fact of the test child's world, :seon.test/candidate {:port :branch}, written in memory at the child's start, which seon.test-store/shared-root! and seon.agent-test/in-agent read from that db: no var, no config, no env; shared-root! answers it for the default arguments and boots no root. OUTPUT: the batch's answer as today ({:results :reports :digests}, verdict-tx written by the prove proc), plus for the switch-over {:binary :digest :commit C :results} (runtime, seon.system/an-update-loses-nothing). PROOFS (reference implementation f91eddfcd0 + 0778373bb2 on jolt/tests2, reverted there; its tests are the proofs, each seen red): (a) seon.test-store-test/a-process-test-shares-the-candidate-its-run-binds: the in-agent harness, handed a candidate, answers it and boots no root; red stand-in: a shared-root! that ignores the candidate (1 fail). (b) seon.agent-test/a-process-item-runs-on-the-candidate-its-batch-carries: a grant carrying a candidate runs its process item, none refused; without one, refused as before; red stand-in: a granted-selection that ignores the candidate (2 fail). (c) the grant tests stay green: seon.agent-test/a-granted-item-is-run-or-answered-with-its-reason, an-overlapping-runs-plan-is-its-own, seon.prove-test/a-grant-of-no-items-runs-nothing. COST: one boot per C (~10.7 s today, target 2 s) plus ~20 ms a child and ~21 ms a test; a selection 956 ticks (result/bc903e68); process tests today 159 of 2,193 through the gate's build (98 s + 55 s). Then the :gate op, ask-gate/send-gate! (flow's rebuild line), seon.fx run-on-checkout, run-held, run-tests, gate-answer and the items path (~330 lines) are deleted in that landing. Cost row: a process test ~20 ms a child plus ~21 ms a test; today 326 process tests sum 482 s through the gate's checkout (98 s build + 55 s warm per candidate) [M result/ca300ce2]. Owner: tests2. The owner's design, confirmed 2026-11-16 (decisions: the system process is the fork point): the binary's main process is the system, single-threaded for life; it loads main by load-diff! of its held db against each commit notice, collects, and forks. Root is its first fork; agents and test children are root's siblings and inherit only what the system process built once. Bound: a forked agent's own memory <=5 MB, no baggage (docs/cost.md#the-test-systems-lower-bound); every READY gives a fresh agent's private bytes after fork, after a collection, after one read, beside 5 MB. Tests: the runtime rail forks from the branch's warm agent; selection is a set operation; results are branch facts. Deletes: bin/test, bin/prove, bin/land (gone), test_runner, the file cache, the results branch, shared roots. DESIGN (tests2 with flow, 2026-11-16): a test child is forked by the system process (the one fork point), never by the branch's agent: an agent is multithreaded (Flow fibers, effect workers), fork() copies one thread and inherits the others' held locks, and today seon.agent/fork-test-child is refused (:seon.test/fork-refused) whenever another thread is active. The child, forked at main's program, runs load-diff! of its branch's changed forms (O(d), ms a form), then its tests on world values (d/with): no disk, no checkout, never collecting what it inherited. Cost: a fork ~1-5 ms, d forms, ~21 ms a test. The branch's agent only asks (seon_prove); the fork op is runtime's. OPEN, measured: (1) one judge plan allocates 167-372 MB and keeps the branch graph, base graph and branch changes live in the agent, all private under fork-first: the plan's state becomes structural sharing over the system process's held graph, the agent holding diffs only; (2) each agent's Datahike node cache fills privately (deps); (3) the fixtures' live size in the system process is unmeasured. RULER: the 5 MB rows become a platform test on the candidate binary (fork, read the footprint, collect, read, one read, read, a merge, read), so each READY quotes one repeatable result; the footprint is seon.lib/real-mb (phys_footprint, deps), one reading an eval. THE RULER, interface agreed with runtime and deps (2026-11-16): seon.fork-server-test/a-forked-agent-holds-its-memory-rows, a platform test on the one booted candidate (seon.test-store/candidate answers {:port :system-pid :writer-pid :branch b}, built and booted once a test run; deps), reached through seon.server/ask on :port (:attach, :eval, :prove). It attaches an agent on its own branch b (seon.system/spawn! in the writer process; runtime), reads seon.lib/real-mb in one eval each after the fork, after (System/gc) and after one read of 100 datoms (the read its own eval before the gauge), then makes a program move on b (an eval defining a form) and takes the same three readings from a fresh agent on b; the system process's own by seon.system/fork-server-stats (runtime) before and after. Asserts each agent row <= :seon.config.agent/own-mb (5), and the system process after the move <= its row before plus the moved forms; answers every reading as data on the result, so each READY quotes one run key. Until the rail exists, the same steps run by hand as writer-process code on the standing system. MEASURED by hand, deps' binary, 2026-11-16: the GAUGE is a fork's PRIVATE dirty pages (vmmap's SM=PRV, the kernel's private count by pid: a native call, deps), never phys_footprint, which charges a fork for copy-on-write pages still shared with its parent (runtime: of a fresh agent's 206 MB dirty heap, 165 MB SM=COW, 40.7 MB SM=PRV; tests2, agent pid 63513 after its first eval and two plans: of 370 MB dirty heap, 327 MB COW, 43.7 MB PRV, plus ~21 MB malloc). So a working agent's own memory is ~65 MB against 5 MB, about 13x; the footprint rows first quoted (137, +232, +75 MB) overstate it and are withdrawn. The ruler reads each row on the private gauge, jolt.host/process-private-bytes (deps): each process reads its own private dirty pages (one region walk, a few ms), since macOS gives no process another's task port; each process answers it in its Flow ping (:private-bytes in a proc's :ping-map-fn: the agent's transport proc, the writer process's procs, the system process's one proc), never by an eval, so a reading costs no eval's pages; the ruler pings the three at its fixed points (after fork, after a collection, after one read, across a merge). Linux: /proc/self/smaps_rollup's Private_Dirty. SYSTEM PROCESS ROW, measured 2026-11-16 at the writer process with seon.system/fork-server-stats sys true (runtime's; result/c63b7435): 1,380 MB, 3 OS threads, 1 active; Chez's static heap: strings 140 MB (608k), pairs 117 MB (7.3M), code 76 MB, vectors 49 MB, records 29 MB, rp-info 26 MB, procedures 22 MB, symbols 31 MB. Against the ~120 MB minimum, code and its tables (~110 MB) are the program; the strings and pairs have no line in the minimum and are traced first. The row's test needs a booted system process, which a test reaches only through the platform rail (seon.test-store/candidate), so it is written when that lands. Freed garbage is not kept whole (deps: 200 MB of garbage in a forked child, two collections, 72 MB at Chez's default and 75 MB with release-minimum-generation lowered), so the plan's measured high-water has another cause, to be found on this gauge.\nPLAN, THE REBUILD OF IN-AGENT TESTS (tests2, for root's review, 2026-11-17): the two rails. An in-agent test is a fork of its branch's warm agent on the fast path; only platform tests (boot, restart, the binary, a switch-over, a pin) run on the candidate. PRIOR WORK: my.test/eval-in (src/my/test.clj:110) already runs forms as an agent's test fork on a world value: recorded and noted as an agent's are, writes on the world's value, no root booted (my.test-test/eval-in-records-each-form-as-its-own-tasks-agent). The rebuild extends it; nothing is built beside it. CLASSES (117 tests calling seon.agent-test/in-agent or with-agent, by what they ask of a root, test sources scanned): 40 evaluate forms at their agent only; 12 read its turns (seon.turn/context-block, scripted model); 4 read the store's branch; 61 ask root something: its REPL (26), a server op (attach, done, message, context: 8), or both, or the reconciler. STEPS: (1) the 40, then the 16, rewritten in place onto my.test/eval-in (a turn's read and a branch read on the world value; eval-in gains what they need, each piece proved red first); (2) each of the 61 split at its seam: the root side proved by its pure function on a world (seon.server's op pieces, seon.reconcile/judgment, as done for resume-task and import-checkout), the agent side by eval-in, and only an end-to-end path that needs a real process (spawn, a merge through the writer, a restart) stays a platform test on the candidate, each on a main of its own; (3) seon.agent-test/in-agent and with-agent deleted once nothing calls them. COST, as arithmetic: an eval-in test is a world value (made once a process, ~3.2M ticks) plus its forms, ~0.3M ticks and ~1 MB a form: one to three forms ~0.5-1M ticks, 2-4 MB, ~5 ms; a test child's fork ~20 ms once a batch. Measured at an agent: one form 527k ticks / 1.9 MB / 3.4 ms, three forms 938k / 4.0 MB / 5.5 ms, defn and call 932k / 3.5 MB / 5.0 ms (result/ad471278). Against an in-agent test on the candidate: median 1.26 s, 117 x ~1.26 s ~ 147 s run one at a time (flow's rule until this lands); after step 1, the 56 cost ~0.3 s; the platform tests left are the count step 2 settles, each named with why. The class of red flow found (tests on one candidate root seeing each other's writes) goes with it: each eval-in test has its own world value.\nWHERE IT STANDS (tests2, paused for flow's candidate fix): F2 is live (5842a770bb) but a judge's in-agent process tests still run without a candidate (the child's world holds no :seon.test/candidate, so shared-root! boots its own root: :seon.test-store/no-process-store); flow is fixing it. On main: the candidate read (seon.test-store/candidate-of, 7cca65105f); the done rule holds a reached test left unrun (:unrun, :proof-unknown, 1f573daad5); kept points name committed values and a read's opening page sits at its first read's basis (58c3138704); a later context read costs ~3M ticks, not 1.08B (seon.ctx.wire/moved-since). Waiting on the candidate: prove seon.server-test/a-later-read-draws-no-page-cold and a-reads-kept-points-name-committed-values there (both green 3/3+ at an agent), and seon.proofs' 5 rewritten process tests (38a799b954). Process tests 165, none greeting-only: 110 in-agent on the candidate, 49 forks of the child, 6 own-root.\nTHE FLOW CENSUS (owner, 2026-11-16: every process a Flow process; docs/flow.md#every-process-is-a-flow-process): tests2: test children forked by the system process, each a one-proc graph answering on its socket; the Seon-binary subprocesses of the :tests and :process effects (bin/test shards, jolt test-runner, ./seon --steps, bin/check) deleted by it, with bin/test, bin/prove, bin/full-run and bin/jolt-gates. BATCH 3 (2026-11-17): count 96 -> 95 (server-test/a-swap-of-the-agents-state-answers-its-summary moved, 35f4068b34). What the rest need, from two helpers' reads at agents: TURNS (~16 seon.turn/work! tests, and the my.root/run! ones): under eval-in a turn stops at three: eval-in's state has no :tx-seq (prove! inc's nil); prove! waits on root's test service (seon.agent/await-reply, agent.clj ~296), which forks a test child; the model call goes to root's effects owner (seon.agent/fx! -> fx-test, :seon.fx/in-test unless seon.agent/*fx-stub* is bound). Proposed, one seam set in eval-in: :tx-seq 0 and init!'s state schema meta in its state; *fx-stub* bound to the :perform the system declaration (resources/seon/system.edn :fx) names for the kind, called in this process (the scripted model through seon.fx/model, the real code); a test agent's prove! answered in this process: its run's plan (run-plan), its in-process items run here by seon.proofs' runner, its process tests named deferred. Cost: what the turn's own work costs, no fork, no root. What it gives up: those tests no longer pass through root's prove service and effects owner, which their own tests cover (seon.prove-test, seon.fx-test). SERVER OPS (server-test 14, turn-test 1): {:op :context} is (seon.turn/context-block agent task outside? wire?) at the agent, which runs under eval-in today (needs the real prompt page on conn: seon.doc/load-tx); {:op :eval :agent a} is (seon.turn/outside-reply! agent task msg evals turn?), callable under eval-in, its :out sedn; {:op :message} is (d/transact conn (seon.task/message-tx ...)); {:op :attach} is what eval-in already writes; {:op :eval :agent \"root\"} forms on c/transact-main! become d/transact on conn. Stay platform tests: :op :prove, :op :done, interrupts and kills, spawns, the shared root's own test. REBUILD STATUS (tests2, 2026-11-17): in-agent tests 103 -> 73 (batches 1-4 onto my.test/eval-in and agent_test's turn-in; batch 4, main e95c6d3190, proved 4 of 4 fresh). WORST COST FIRST (median wall ms of their stored runs on main and the results branch, result/c87c0454): a-task-whose-proofs-pass-is-done-only-when-what-it-reaches-is-green 35,399; a-red-at-its-digest-is-reused-with-its-failures 25,369; a-process-test-green-at-its-digest-asks-no-gate 20,952 (stays: its point is a process test's classification); root-reads-the-health-the-render-proc-keeps 20,857; two-test-runs-at-once-each-read-their-child-to-its-end 10,251 (stays: test children); seon.repl-test/the-template-instructions-and-the-help-page-run 8,977; the rest under 7.4 s. The top four cost 102 s, as much as the next 40. STAYING by what it tests (forked children, a process death, a send to another process, a global redefinition, root's ops): 14 of the 22 that call no root op, classified 2026-11-17. DONE 2026-11-17: a-task-whose-proofs-pass-is-done-only-when-what-it-reaches-is-green (35 s -> 0.34 s, main 86e85446d2: its change breaks the test branch's own app.stray, never seon.repl) and a-red-at-its-digest-is-reused-with-its-failures (25 s -> 18 ms of judge, main 0098e14ec7) run as test agents: 71 left. STAY by what they test: a-process-test-green-at-its-digest-asks-no-gate (a process test's classification), root-reads-the-health-the-render-proc-keeps (root's render proc and error fold; its 21 s is root's whole health reads, not the agent), two-test-runs-at-once-each-read-their-child-to-its-end and the test-child tests (children), seon.repl-test/the-template-instructions-and-the-help-page-run (every help form in a real agent: my.turn/wait, my.task/fail!, merge-main!). NEXT: the rest are under 3 s each and call root's ops (:context, :attach, a root REPL eval), fork children or need an agent process; what moves them is a server op answered in this process for a test agent (H2's batch 3 finding: the op's function called with the test's conn as main), else each stays on its shared root. DESIGN FOR THE REST, for root's reading before any build (tests2, 2026-11-17). WHAT THEY USE (the 71, by their bodies): 36 send a root REPL :eval whose forms call root's procs on sys: seon.system/submit! to the agent (10), call! to the writer (9), transact-main! and await-db (6 each), my.root/run! the reconciler (6), kill! (5), problems, message!, prove!; the other ops are :attach (6), :write and :branch-db (5 each), :done and :context (4 each), :spawn (3). So they need root's procs (the api, the writer, the reconciler), not a few op answers; a copy of each op's answer for a test would be the second path. PRIOR WORK: seon.server/op-answer, one method an op over sys (server.clj ~370); seon.system's spawn!, which forks the agent through the fork server and links it by jolt.async.remote over a socket; my.test/eval-in, an agent in this process (role :test, its effects named by the test, its prove in-process: run-here); seon.test-store/shared-root!, a root process booted once a shard, each test a branch of its store as its main. THE ONE PATH: the same procs run in the test's process on the test's own stored branch as main (ts/test-branch!: a pointer write), and spawn's process boundary is the one switch: an agent spawned in-process is eval-in's test agent, its link a pair of local channels where remote's socket is, so c/submit!, c/state, kill! and every op method reach it as they reach a forked one. A test's root forms are evaluated in this process with sys bound to that system. Nothing else is test-only: the ops, the writer, the reconciler and the judge are the running code. COST: today each of these tests pays its agent's fork and process (~1.5M ticks of setup and teardown here, an agent process of 262-640 MB on the candidate root, deps' measure) and every ask a round trip to the shared root; the design pays one in-process system a shard (the procs' start, no binary boot: to be measured, estimated under one shared root's boot) and per test a branch (~1M) and an in-process agent (~1M, as eval-in's), its asks function calls. WHAT STAYS a process test: the tests whose claim is a process (a forked child, a death, a signal, the binary, a socket), by what they test. FIRST STEP: measure the procs' in-process start on a test branch, and move one root-:eval test (a-woken-agent-is-not-sent-its-own-loop) seen red under a stand-in; then the rest by cost."
