 {:seon.task/name "a-turns-prove-answers-its-size-not-its-deferred-list", :seon.task/kind :work, :seon.task/proofs #{seon.server-test/a-prove-answers-its-counts-and-reds-not-its-deferred-names}, :seon.task/estimate #:seon.server{op-answer {:n 1000, :order :linear, :ticks 1000000, :bytes 10000000}}, :seon.task/plan "Found by data in the first real seon_done (2026-11-15) [M]: seon_prove for one agent ran 1 test (green) and answered 77,389 characters, 1,425 lines, almost all of it :deferred, the names of the process tests left to the judge; the MCP client refused it as past its token bound, so a session reads it only from a saved file. What a reader needs is the counts and the reds; the deferred names are a query of the run (its key). PRIOR WORK: seon.prove's answer shape, seon.server's :prove op (```seon.server/op-answer```, docs/server.md#ops). Fix: the answer holds counts by status, the reds, and the run's key; the deferred names are read by a query of that run. COST: answer O(reds), not O(deferred)."}
 {:seon.task/name "a-pin-bump-runs-its-in-process-tests-on-its-candidate", :seon.task/kind :work, :seon.task/estimate #:seon.agent{process-plan {:n 10, :order :linear, :ticks 20000, :bytes 100000}}, :seon.task/proofs #{seon.prove-test/a-pin-bump-runs-its-in-process-tests-on-its-candidate}, :seon.task/plan "Owner: flow (root, 2026-11-17, after a433b4ab73). CAUSE: a branch that moves a native pin (deps' lmdb fix) has its in-process tests run in its agent, which runs on the standing binary and so on the old library. The fix is then judged by the old library's red: deps' done parked in 6 s, reusing that red. Schemas were data, and the registry in force solved them; a pin is native code, so data cannot solve it. A second gap on the same path: seon.agent/moved-input? reads only files (seon.source/changed-files), and a pin is a :seon.pin/pin-fact entity, never a file. So a pure pin bump reads as no input moved, and even its process tests boot on the standing binary. PRIOR WORK: searched seon.agent/process-plan, moved-input?, write-out-of; seon.source/pins-of; seon.program's env-keys and pin-reads; seon.writer/write-out-req; the tasks naming :seon.pin. Found: an in-process test already takes a pin into its digest by reach (seon.program/pin-reads, program.clj:1796), and a process test takes every pin (env-keys, program.clj:2284). So the digest is right and only where the test runs is wrong. process-plan already builds a candidate from the branch's written-out files when an input moved (:build :write-out), and write-out-of already writes the pins that differ from base's. FIX, extending process-plan and creating no new mechanism: (1) an input moved when changed-files hits :seon.config.build/inputs, or when (not= (source/pins-of base) (source/pins-of db)). Step (1) is done: seon.agent/input-moved? (files or pins) is what process-plan and an agent's start read (seon.agent-test/a-pin-bump-moves-a-build-input). (2) When a pin moved, the in-process tests run-plan selects to run go to the candidate as items beside the process tests (process-plan's :process gains them at their digests), and none of them runs in the agent. Then their verdicts come only from the binary that holds the pin. The red already stored at deps' digest is never deleted (a red is reused only by the branch that ran it). At build, check whether deps' branch still reuses it; if it does, the verdict's key must also name where it ran, the candidate's build, so a run on the old binary is never the new one's verdict. DELETES: nothing; the agent's in-process run on a pin branch is replaced by the candidate's. COST: pins-of is one :aevt seek over about 10 pin datoms on each of two dbs, about 20k ticks per plan, paid by every plan. A pin branch pays one write-out and build (117 s measured for the write-out end to end), one boot (7 to 15 s) and its reached tests once, the same test work as in the agent; no other branch pays anything more. WIDENED (root, 2026-11-17: a branch moving any build input has its tests of the bake run where the bake is the branch's). Two designs, costed. (A) All of a build-input branch's reached in-process tests on its candidate: a schema file reaches about 1,600 tests, and every turn of a schema-changing branch would pay a write-out and build (117 s), a boot (7 to 15 s) and lose the agent's warm fork. Rejected: the cost lands on every turn. (B) Only the tests that read the bake: by reach that is no narrower, because 2,210 tests reach seon.config/schema-forms through the contracts (result/de6860b6), and the direct readers are about ten test files, a hand-made list. Rejected. CHOSEN: fix the bake split at the data model, as the registry in force fixed it for contracts (cce54b0de2). seon.config/keys-declared already reads the branch's held schema, while seon.config/schema-forms (and seon.contract/forms, the same value) is the binary's bake, so a branch adding a key has it declared with no form: seon.config-test/every-key-is-documented-by-its-schema and the-schema-forms-are-baked-once went red on runtime's branch while main held them green (result/beeb1d41). The forms in force are the bake with the held branch's schema changes merged in (seon.program/schema-changes, the same changes registry-with compiles), read where keys-declared reads; O(changed schema entries). Then a schema branch's in-process tests are right in its agent, and only a pin (native code, which data cannot carry) sends its reached in-process tests to the candidate. MEASURE at build: a pin bump's plan before and after (:input-moved?, where the reached tests run), and deps' lmdb branch judged green on its candidate. PROOF: seon.prove-test/a-pin-bump-runs-its-in-process-tests-on-its-candidate, red first by a stand-in that keeps the in-process items in the agent."}
